    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_3e3a061cd486868d757f8152ebbf6aed547d908c3c5e5ba0c8e4e722e0564edf",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source connects auxiliary-function design through two different objective regimes. An admissible trigonometric polynomial supplies b0, b1, and b and fixes theta; theta in turn determines the compactly supported autoconvolution smoother and the decomposition F(z)=f(0)/z+F0(z), whose bounded remainder enters the finite-height error budget. After Y and Y1 vanish asymptotically, the leading coefficient objective R2 remains, whereas the all-height objective R1 retains secondary terms. This makes the reported degree-46 R2 winner and degree-40 R1 winner a concrete consequence of optimizing different proof interfaces, not merely two mentions of polynomial search; the separate classical-region rerun also shows that changing the smoothing family changes a finite-height constant while holding other named ingredients fixed.",
