    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_fd3e9c936d7bab8ba47b5457a2d6e090aec4269e5a0dec4c6ad2a4d858c3d76b",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The intermediate-region proof and the all-height contradiction form a producer-consumer interface that the future-work section generalizes. The fixed-line argument supplies a finite-height zero-free boundary; the main contradiction consumes that boundary on the short starting interval to certify the empty rectangle required by its detector, then uses monotone error bounds to exclude a counterexample at every larger height. Replacing this single handoff by successive boundaries nu_j yields an envelope construction in which each finite certificate supplies the precondition for the next stage.",
